# Game commentary source.

# Formatting:
# [*LINK]: Jump to a new note; also display source if possible. (Default behavior.)
# [~LINK]: Display source for LINK, but keep this note open.
# [text|*LINK], [text|~LINK]: Specify link text.
# [text|https://...] External link.

ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!

rtn:V-VERSION: Print the title, release, and serial number.
	This version of Zork 3 is older than the versions of Zork 1 and 2 on this site. It doesn't have the [ZORK-NUMBER|relative:../zork1/#GLOB:ZORK-NUMBER] constant yet.


GRAMMAR-LEGEND: This is the list of verb forms which the game recognizes. (Simple directions like `NORTH` are a special case; they’re not listed here.) These are compiled from the [SYNTAX|~src:SYNTAX-63] file.
	Objects in a verb line have various flags which give hints about their usage. An object attribute (like `LIGHTBIT`) means that if the player doesn’t name an object, the parser will try to guess one with that attribute.
	The other flag letters have specific meanings: the object must be **h**eld; the object will be **t**aken if not held; the object should be **i**n the room or **o**n the ground; the object should be **p**ossessed or **c**arried in a container. (Yes, they’re confusing. Infocom’s own docs say “No one really understands them except Stu” (Galley).)

attr:RMUNGBIT: Not applied to real objects. `RMUNGBIT` is a hack used in grammar lines; it means the object is optional (but the preposition before it is required). This allows the parser to accept commands like [LOOK AROUND|~src:SYNTAX-283].

glob:P-SYNTAX: The grammar line found when the most recent command was parsed. (Except for simple direction commands, which are a special case.) See the Grammar tab.


OBJTREE-LEGEND: In this list, `Rooms` are rooms and `Items` are regular items. (Sometimes portable, sometimes not.)
	`Scen` indicates scenery objects. These exist in multiple places; for example, the [~obj:STAIRS] are visible from the entrance and every other room that mentions “stairs”.
	`Glob` indicates a global object, which is *always* visible, or at least mentionable. For example, [~obj:GRUE] is global – not because grues are always present, but so you can always ask `WHAT IS A GRUE`.

obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS: This is a container for scenery objects like [~obj:STAIRS], which are visible from many locations in the game.


src:DUNGEON-24: This refers to the word `MGCKJK` in [~obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS], above.
	Why is this synonym necessary? Does it need to be alphabetically last? Not clear! Most Infocom games have it (or a variation), but some say “No, this synonym doesn’t need to exist” instead.
	If you type `EXAMINE MGCKJK`, the parser finds [~obj:local-globals]. But that object is nameless, so you get a garbage reply. Go on, try it.

obj:ADVENTURER: Yes, the player’s printed name is “cretin”, although the game rarely says so. (The error for boarding a vehicle you’re already in mentions it.) This sort of sarcastic insult hasn’t aged too well, but MIT culture had a lot of it. Compare [*glob:WINNER], and also the old MIT [Jargon File|https://jargon-file.org/archive/jargon-1.0.0.33.dos.txt].
	Note that if you refer to `ME` (or `CRETIN`), you get the [*obj:ME] global object, not the `ADVENTURER`.
	The [prop:STRENGTH] property is not used in this game.

obj:ME: A global object which is always in scope. Commands referring to `ME` hit this, rather than [*obj:ADVENTURER], which is a stub that moves around the map.
	Because of this, parser messages mostly refer to you as “you”, even though the [*obj:ADVENTURER]’s printed name is “cretin”.

glob:PLAYER: `PLAYER` is always the [~obj:ADVENTURER]. Compare [*glob:WINNER].

glob:WINNER: `WINNER` is the actor in the current command. (Compare [*glob:PLAYER].) 
	`WINNER` is usually [~obj:ADVENTURER], but for a command like `MAN, GO EAST` it shifts to the named NPC.
	“Winner” is just as sarcastic as “[cretin|*obj:ADVENTURER]”, but from the other side.

glob:HERE: The location of the [*glob:WINNER].

glob:LIT: Whether the current location has light. Set by calling the [rtn:LIT?] routine.

rtn:LIT?: Determine whether a given location has light. This is true if the room or anything in it has [attr:ONBIT].
	This routine is used to set the [glob:LIT] variable.

glob:LOW-DIRECTION: Properties with this value and above are directions.

attr:ONBIT: Set for glowing objects and naturally-illuminated rooms.
	This is different from [attr:LIGHTBIT], which indicates objects that the player can `TURN ON`. See also [attr:FLAMEBIT], objects which are on fire.

attr:FLAMEBIT: Set for objects which are on fire. Such objects normally also have [attr:ONBIT].
	This is different from [attr:BURNBIT], which indicates objects that the player can `BURN`.

attr:LIGHTBIT: Set for objects which the player can sensibly `TURN ON/OFF` (e.g. the [~obj:lamp]).
	This is different from [attr:ONBIT], which indicates objects that are currently lit up. See also [attr:BURNBIT], objects which the player can `BURN`.

attr:BURNBIT: Set for objects which the player can `BURN`. (It might be a bad idea, but you can do it.)
	This is different from [attr:FLAMEBIT], which indicates objects that are currently on fire. See also [attr:LIGHTBIT], objects which the player can `TURN ON`.

attr:RLANDBIT: Notionally this is for rooms which are solid ground. But Zork 3 doesn’t make use of this; every room has `RLANDBIT`, even [~obj:ON-LAKE] and [~obj:IN-LAKE].

prop:VALUE: Only the [~OBJ:SWORD] has this property, and Zork 3 never uses it.

prop:STRENGTH: Only the [~OBJ:ADVENTURER] has this property, and Zork 3 never uses it.

attr:VILLAIN: Generally set for living beings, although they are generally not villainous in Zork 3. The [SWORD|~rtn:INFESTED?] reacts to this flag.
	Compare [~attr:VICBIT].
	The [~obj:ADVENTURER] has the `VILLAIN` flag, which leads to a few buggy messages. Try `HELLO ME` or `WAKE ME`, for example. (The sword-glow avoids this, at least, since the `ADVENTURER` is also `INVISIBLE`.)

attr:VICBIT: Generally set for living beings, though not the [~obj:ADVENTURER].
	This is almost the same as [~attr:VILLAIN], but some verbs use [~attr:VILLAIN] and some `VICBIT`, with no obvious pattern.
	Interestingly, the [~obj:MIRROR] has `VICBIT`, possibly to support the idea of attacking and breaking it. This leads to a few buggy messages, though. (Try `GIVE LAMP TO MIRROR`, for example.)

glob:SPRAYED?: Whether you’ve been sprayed with grue repellant.

glob:P-WON: Set if the parser successfully parsed a command. (As opposed to a blank line or an error.)
	No relation to whether the *player* has won.

glob:WON-FLAG: In other Zorks, this flag is set when you win the game. In Zork 3 it is unused.

glob:LUCKY: In Zork 3, you are always lucky.

glob:C-INTS: Indicates how much of the timer table ([*glob:C-TABLE]) is used. This counts down from `C-TABLELEN`, which is 180.

glob:C-DEMONS: Indicates how much of the timer table is used for daemons.
	The difference between daemons and timers is that timers run every turn; daemons run every *input*, even if no turn passes. But Zork 3 does not use daemons.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the “Timers” tab for a current listing.

TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many turns. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.
	Note that enabled negative timers, like [*rtn:I-SWORD], count down towards “negative infinity”. They’ll wrap and stop working after 65535 turns, but back then nobody worried about this!

rtn:GO: Set up the world at the start of the game. Start the timers. Print the banner. Set all those useful globals like [~glob:HERE] and [~glob:PLAYER].

rtn:ZPROB: This function is never called.
	Every die-roll in Zork 3 uses the one-argument form of [PROB|~src:MACROS-66]. That’s compiled as an inlined `@RANDOM` opcode, not `ZPROB`.
	`ZPROB` has extra logic to decrease your chance of success if you are un-[glob:LUCKY]. But nothing in the game makes you un-[glob:LUCKY], so this is never needed.

src:VERBS-1080: This message can’t be printed; it’s the second `ELSE` clause in a row. In fact the ZIL compiler recognizes this and skips compiling this string.


rtn:I-LANTERN: This routine sits in the timers table. When it fires, it prints a “lantern has gotten dimmer” message and re-queues itself for the next dimming stage. (Until the lantern goes out entirely, of course.) The messages themselves (and their durations) are found in [*glob:LAMP-TABLE].

glob:LAMP-TABLE: This table shows the successive stages of the lamp going out. The *first* stage is 200 turns, which is set in [~rtn:GO], not here.

glob:CURRENT-LAMP: The current form of your lantern. This may be the [~obj:FRIED-LAMP] or the [~obj:BROKEN-LAMP], depending on how careless you’ve been.
	When you die, the `CURRENT-LAMP` is returned to you. If you died in the dark because you busted it, sucks to be you.

obj:SWORD: Still Glamdring and/or Orcrist after all these years. (Ironically, not Excalibur.)
	The [prop:VALUE] property is not used in this game.

rtn:I-SWORD: This routine runs every turn that you are carrying the [obj:SWORD]. It checks adjacent rooms for enemies and displays the “sword is glowing” message.
	The sword’s glow level is stored in the [glob:SWORD-STATE] variable.
	See [this note|src:SHADOW-460], however.

glob:SWORD-STATE: The glow level. Set by [rtn:I-SWORD].
	See [this note|src:SHADOW-460], however.

src:SHADOW-460: When the [shadow arrives|~rtn:SHADOW-ARRIVAL], your sword appears, “wildly glowing”. It doesn’t start the [rtn:I-SWORD] timer, however. The sword’s glow feature doesn’t actually work until the next time you drop it and pick it up.
	(c:Torbjörn Andersson)

glob:SHADOW-DIAG: When the [~obj:SHADOW]’s strength is 3, they are described as “probably not capable of hindering your movement”. This is not really true; the [shadow blocks you|~src:SHADOW-426] with no strength check. But since the shadow only blocks one random direction in the wide-open Land of Shadow, it doesn’t really matter.

glob:P-STRENGTH: The player’s combat strength (and health).
	The [~obj:ADVENTURER] has a [prop:STRENGTH] property, but this is unused.

glob:S-STRENGTH: The [~obj:SHADOW]’s combat strength (and health).

glob:ATTACK-MODE: Whether the [~obj:SHADOW] is actively attacking you. They don't start the moment they arrive; it's a [30% chance per turn|~src:SHADOW-433], or when [you attack them|~rtn:SHADOW-ATTACK].


rtn:V-SCORE: The score message is an understated piece of narrative framing.
	Zork 1 was a 350-point game; Zork 2 was 400 points. Practically the first thing I did when jumping into Zork 3 was to type `SCORE` and say “...[Seven|~glob:SCORE-MAX] points?” Followed by “Your *potential*?”
	This is an up-front hint that you have seven specific goals – perhaps seven major treasures rather than a grab-bag of generically shiny stuff.
	(The game *does* have seven “treasures”, but as it turns out, the points are not awarded for collecting them. See [glob:SCORE], [rtn:LOOK-LIKE-DM?].)

glob:SCORE: Zork 3 has [seven points|~glob:SCORE-MAX], but unlike the first two Zorks, they are not awarded for solving puzzles or collecting treasures. As a rule, you receive points for *discovering* puzzles. Solving each puzzle provides an *item*, which may or may not look treasure-y.
	See [rtn:V-SCORE], [rtn:LOOK-LIKE-DM?].

rtn:LOOK-LIKE-DM?: Checks whether you have the seven critical items. Redundant with [rtn:DMISH], really.

rtn:DMISH: Counts how many of the seven critical items you have. Redundant with [rtn:LOOK-LIKE-DM?], really.

glob:BASE-SCORE: You do not lose points for [dying|glob:DEATHS], so `BASE-SCORE` is unused.

glob:DEATHS: Unlike in previous Zorks, there is no penalty for dying until the [fourth death|~src:VERBS-294]. Nonetheless, it is understood that dying is a bad idea! No puzzle in Zork 3 requires dying, although of course you will die a lot while experimenting to solve them.
	(The [Altar scene|~rtn:ZORK-IV-F] provides a preview of an *Enchanter* puzzle where you *do* have to die! Sort of.)


src:SHADOW-1305: The hungry fish and the roc will reappear in *Spellbreaker*. (That future scene is described as the “Flathead Ocean”, so it’s *probably* not this lake. It’s clearly inspired by it, though.)

src:SHADOW-1384: The roc and the hungry fish will reappear in *Spellbreaker*. (That future scene is described as the “Flathead Ocean”, so it’s *probably* not this lake. It’s clearly inspired by it, though.)


rtn:I-MAN-APPEARS: Chest Guy is the most complex scripted sequence in the Zork trilogy. (Although not Infocom’s first; *Deadline* was released before Zork 3.) It involves a chain of *five* timed events: [~rtn:I-MAN-APPEARS], [~rtn:I-MAN-PRESENT], [~rtn:I-MAN-RETURNS], [~rtn:I-MAN-LIFT], [~rtn:I-MAN-LEAVES]. The sequence has to account for several off-ramps and end-conditions. (You could leave at any point, carry the chest away, etc.)

obj:BROKEN-STAFF: The game teases you with the idea that you could steal the [~obj:VALUABLES], but anything you try leads to breaking the staff.
	(Or does it? Two options are covered: [attacking the man|~src:SHADOW-1202] and [throwing away the staff|~src:SHADOW-1538] *before* attacking the man. But Infocom often had object-manipulation bugs. Is there a way to wind up with both the staff and the valuables? Visible Zorker fan challenge!)


src:TM-900: The comment implies that if you steal the jewels, the museum would be abandoned earlier: year 777 ([~const:YEAR-CAGED]) instead of 883 ([~glob:YEAR-CLOSED]). This change is not implemented, however.

const:REAL-YEAR-CLOSED: This constant is never used. It might have been intended to support the “changed history” idea noted [here|src:TM-900].

rtn:JIGS-UP: This routine handles your demise and possible resurrection. A few particularly nasty deaths use [rtn:REALLY-DEAD] instead.

src:VERBS-280: The Dungeon Master never resurrects you if you have left your [home year|~const:YEAR-PRESENT]. (Death by museum guard goes through [rtn:REALLY-DEAD], which doesn’t even bother to check this, since the guards only exist in the past.)
	Is the Dungeon Master unable to travel through time? Does he not exist in the past or future? Fertile ground for theological debate.

src:VERBS-309: This paragraph is the player’s only clue to what objects are needed to [imitate the DM|~rtn:LOOK-LIKE-DM?]. It’s deliberately incomplete, which is harsh by modern standards.
	(There is a [full description|~src:ACTIONS-1340] of the DM later, but you can’t get it until you’ve passed the [rtn:LOOK-LIKE-DM?] test.)

rtn:REALLY-DEAD: Like [rtn:JIGS-UP], except with no hope of resurrection. (Or of `RESTART` or `RESTORE`, either, which is a bit rude.) This fate is reserved for dying in the past or annoying the Dungeon Master.


src:SHADOW-1568: This code is indeed copied straight from [Zork 1|relative:../zork1/#OBJ:LADDER-TOP], complete with the comments that indicate how Zork 1 was copied from MIT Zork.

glob:EMPTY-HANDED: This is only checked for the passage between the [~obj:TIMBER-ROOM] and the [~obj:LOWER-SHAFT]. Therefore it is only computed when in those two rooms. See [~rtn:NO-OBJS].

obj:ZORK-IV: Infocom had a running argument, or running gag, about whether *Enchanter* was really “Zork IV” or not.

rtn:ZORK-IV-F: I’ve never been clear on whether this scene was a deliberate preview of the Altar area in *Enchanter*, or whether this was invented freehand and then *Enchanter* designed around it.


glob:MDIR: The orientation of the [~obj:COMPASS-ARROW] (and thus the entire mirror box), in degrees.

src:ACTIONS-1183: By a quirk of ZIL, this line returns the value of [~glob:MDIR] after setting it. Unfortunately, [~rtn:PERFORM] takes a zero return value to mean “action was not handled.” This leads to a spurious “doesn’t work” message when the box rotates to zero degrees (north).


glob:PNUMB: The number displayed on the parapet [~obj:SUNDIAL].

glob:LCELL: The position of the dungeon cell. (This changes to [glob:PNUMB] when you push the [~obj:DIAL-BUTTON].)


